3 #uuid "da1ff81d-d04e-52ec-ab32-281987508598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098809 #edb/ref 1080 "images/13/1309614901d95ce5ff95309d1614987e9f321749e2a64a4b77408101a4746a81.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098809 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098809 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "006ea1df-160d-5e03-a362-bff668361f90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098810 #edb/ref 1080 "images/fd/fdc7faf421e1e594ab729eb741c8f5c68bcb61924e73f2252509d8074a03035c.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098810 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098810 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a493576-895f-56ef-b37c-f24370c67f4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098811 #edb/ref 1080 "images/04/0435b377e5a94d3659c30434be555921d4832e3f441fc9ad2f1092972716d0e7.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098811 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098811 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2b50950d-3c8b-5932-8bd9-48f723083326" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098812 #edb/ref 1080 "images/a2/a232b4460f93da3f75520565625878e71ddd3bc84d86e3d555500af05d165df1.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098812 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098812 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f82dbe0-2877-5330-9eae-afd65254d807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100708 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4979" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100708 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/30/303b6a70330995c124cdfea0bc125febd5ebcf80cb79c8656086d57f3a6f272f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100708 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9857541-376a-5256-b302-74346878af3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100708 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{2}f(x),$ we take the graph of $y = f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y = f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71b6584d644419a56b2cebb64124510e9f15cca8a4f19e6c1a8d4b6f1441cd0a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100709 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100709 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=4f(x).$\n\n![](images/49/495e5e02f89dfa125cb95095088cb80cf3e83ab9e6dae619494e900cbe1dc346.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100709 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "780bc2f7-da0f-5615-92cd-3a9733b3c333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100709 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y = 4f(x),$ we take the graph of $y = f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y = f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b20c1355ae0b2e8afc7e7ce701662e0aaf2a6fdcd8e43342ae057e8bd9250e9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100711 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100711 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-2, 2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Find a point that must lie on the graph of $y= 7f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100711 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "446b51d8-1ec7-532c-9b53-21aeb32d57a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100711 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $y = 7f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y = f(x)$ by a factor of $7$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,2)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $\\left(-2, 7 \\cdot 2\\right) = (-2 , 14),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y = 7f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100985 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100985 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100985 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100985 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1