ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1045 "Vertical Stretches of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101092 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "35b6b482-18e8-514d-960d-4d6d3def3f54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101246 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101246 #edb/ref 1069 "q-17508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101246 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101246 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9a/9a6425ad44f0330287193b8495260033697d862c2a7ce336ac44aeb5bab783fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=2f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101246 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d980652-88c1-5914-8198-5bba18185fc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101246 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y=2f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/a1/a1aa0219a3afeb77d4b04aab0232c1fb75541564fca2291a46d3700831236d16.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101850 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101850 #edb/ref 1061 457106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101850 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4c40517-5865-5128-af8f-564d6a22648d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101850 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101851 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101851 #edb/ref 1061 457107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101851 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "60cd62a7-4991-57ac-90fb-b574ced67839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101851 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101852 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101852 #edb/ref 1061 457110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101852 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea2e9c61-fcab-5300-8818-9590ecbb72e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101852 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101853 #edb/ref 1061 457113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101853 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c481045b-1f32-5a1d-8b62-25e89a1b9393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101853 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101970 #edb/ref 1064 "When we multiply a function by a constant, the graph either stretches out or compresses. Transforming a function in this way is called **scaling**.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below.\n\n![](images/21/21f9b06e470ca0e94605eb72c5a12d2a1f2ec56645588a960f12be81ad8252ec.png)\n\nThe following table lists four sets of points. The set of points in the left-hand column correspond to the graph of $y=f(x),$ and the set on the right-hand side corresponds to $y=2f(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math]} & \\text{Points on [math]y=2f(x)[/math]} \\\\ (1,2) & (1,4) \\\\ (4,-3) & (4,-6) \\\\ (0,2) & (0,4) &  \\\\ (-2,0) & (-2,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x),$ there is a point on the graph of $y=2f(x)$ with the same $x$-value and *double* the $y$-value. \n\nNow that we have four points on the graph of $y=2f(x),$ let's plot the full graph.\n\n![](images/26/262b6515b995f6fdcf7b959dc62d9599b71476471fbc2bde394dcebfd47cabc0.png)\n\nWe say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ **vertically stretched** by a **scale factor** of $2.$ \n\nWe can also say that the graph of $y=2f(x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ stretched by a factor of $2$ **parallel to the $\\mathbf y$-axis.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101970 #edb/ref 1065 3284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101970 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bea7d899-701e-53ab-9bb8-42e452f0f5c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101970 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528298 #edb/ref 1080 "(1,-1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528298 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528298 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00281f2f-3751-58c9-b3d0-f40bdf637ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528299 #edb/ref 1080 "images/0e/0e320f1c1e873ef199547a2b52ccbb63d2e72e8472da1a202f10a7d5a4f1db04.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528299 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528299 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00489729-6e18-5433-800f-f7406a4ba1bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528307 #edb/ref 1080 "images/db/dbd98697b2f9e2586972258e9d7514dcd70201c9968878226c0c5968edf78e87.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528307 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528307 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "034d60dd-9303-51e6-b82e-a264e93f31db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528308 #edb/ref 1080 "images/bd/bdc10d92585b597832f18ba97c914733e4a980e09e9abbe2114d90027c934cbb.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528308 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528308 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03fb57b8-5578-5585-bcd6-9a7c9ac44179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528332 #edb/ref 1080 "(3,2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528332 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528332 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0de34e6e-ce64-5552-8a5c-f69eb0490ff9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528338 #edb/ref 1080 "images/cb/cbd4ac741abc77c6bef177d10e4b0289c5af9cca6cb63a5131f67d0563e4c33d.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528338 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186528338 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11404e03-5065-5a00-9c70-e6cf6344f30d" #edb/ref 17592