 and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4b0c34ba1ed9036d8f42693e1c087f10df75e7fe27b1ad23d8885467de1cfab.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186528949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529103 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b8/b8150a85386a2ebea9119820d25f227a6cb33aef8dd09b519feef4e8f70a4013.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=2f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "257d80f9-98d7-5f29-b46f-5e7f007c2b82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529103 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y=2f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a88c2321811017dcedf2c83178b47dcf8f314926e1fb2ef4273681def639f2a0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529105 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111185" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529105 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529105 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,6)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2708eacc-b12f-5049-bb93-6886b57484fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529105 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)$ represents a compression of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,6)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(3,\\frac{6}{3})=(3,2),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529131 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529131 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82042" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529131 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529131 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/12/12b20f973eee2011e128c27fcf529570a2fa28c892765206ca12395cd3b3774d.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529131 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "613bc0f6-6251-58fe-b528-1093bbbb500f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529131 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y=\\frac{1}{4}f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed813305ba5087c0b70c6756c2a0b3c7c1957fad1a902e7b1c2540d4f727f3b6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529183 #edb/ref 1069 "q-26451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529183 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529183 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/66/66d9e6755720befa3887a3c0e65833f396ce7442ae04f00c3d82721cfc804947.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfd0113c-14f1-5d6a-ac3b-dca77d3d998d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529183 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and compress it by factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of dividing the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe resulting graph is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cfa455809238b0d89c1b496283f6a8be602fa481e98a5e1d8ac72c48346f3703.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529186 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186529046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529186 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529186 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529186 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,-3)$ lies on the curve $y=3f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529186 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1e8554d-3b9d-57c5-a60f-771660835934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529186 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $y=3f(x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $y$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nThe point $Q(3,q)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $P(3,-3)$ on $y=3f(x).$ Since we know that the $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $3$ times the $y$-coordinate of $Q,$ we can form the following equation:\n\n$$\n3q=-3\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get $q=-1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(3,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
